  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1042 PM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nRain and snow showers will continue to work across the Divide early \nWednesday morning, as the storm system lifts out onto the Plains. \nMountain passes will remain slippery overnight, but drier conditions \nand clearing skies will improve road conditions by mid day \nWednesday. Clouds will begin to clear out in the afternoon and \ntemperatures will only recovery slightly as we remain a few degrees \nbelow normal. Winds will remain gusty against the Divide through the \nmorning, then lighten from southwest to northeast as the upper \nlevel winds subside.\n\nA brief transitory ridge moves overhead Thursday, pushing \ntemperatures back up a few degrees. Unfortunately, clouds will begin \nto invade again, ahead of a low diving out of the PACNW into the \nGreat Basin. Winds will begin to pick up again across the Four \nCorners on Thursday, as a strong jet streak rounds the base of the \nlow digging into the Great Basin. By Thursday afternoon, the \nUncompahgre and San Juans will see gusty winds too. Thursday \nevening, another plume of moisture will work into northeast UT and \nnorthwest CO, with rain and snow showers developing. Snow \nlevels drop into the 6-7k neighborhood Thursday night and \ncontinue to lower Friday, with most of the CWA below freezing by\nSaturday morning. Mountain snow will pick up in the Uintas \nThursday night and spread southeast across the CO mountains \nthrough Saturday morning. A few low valleys might see a dusting \nby Saturday morning, should cold air hold with this system. \nOtherwise, it looks like some of the best snow will favor the \ncentral mountains of Colorado. This forecast could vary as the \ntrack of the low adjusts, but for now it looks like a broad \nswath of 6-8 inches of mountain snow and locally higher totals \non favorable aspects.\n\nCold air in the wake of this system will keep afternoon highs below \nnormal through the extended forecast, with morning lows below \nfreezing. The latest freeze on record for Grand Junction's Walker \nField sits at November 26th. In an anomalously warm and wet \nfall, it looks like this record will live on another year \nthanks to the arrival of cold air in the valley with this storm.\nHigh pressure moves across the region late this weekend and \nstarts off Thanksgiving week. Models are hinting at another \ndisturbance crossing the region Tuesday, with perhaps, the \nbeginnings of another unsettled period for the holiday weekend. \nMore on that to come in subsequent forecast packages.",
